<.page title="Nodes" description="How Edge Agent turns a machine into an Edge Admin node.">
  <article class="guide-content guide-topic">
    <a class="back-link" href="/">← Guide overview</a>
    <p class="kicker">Concept 02</p><h1>Nodes are the machines you operate.</h1>
    <p class="lede">A node is an Edge Agent installation registered in a cluster. It is addressed through the cluster mesh, not through a public IP or host SSH port.</p>
    <section><h2>Registration is the handoff</h2><p>An enrollment key joins Edge Agent to a cluster's WireGuard mesh. On first boot, the Agent generates a UUID, persists it in its data volume, and registers through <code>POST /api/v1/agents/nodes/register</code>. Existing installations use the authenticated <code>POST /api/v1/agents/nodes/reregister</code> action.</p><p>If the Agent data volume is lost, an optional one-use recovery key can explicitly restore the previous node identity during initial registration. The recovery key is tied to the node's cluster, is consumed after successful recovery, and is cleared when the node moves to another cluster.</p></section>
    <section><h2>A node is more than a record</h2><p>It is the endpoint for commands, metrics, diagnostics, aliases, centralized SSH credentials, and network access through the proxy. The node ID identifies the Agent installation, not a machine-level identity; losing the Agent volume creates a new node ID unless an explicit recovery key is used.</p></section>
    <section class="next"><h2>Use it</h2><p>Start by listing nodes in Swagger after an agent enrolls. Node detail tells you its cluster, health, installation identity, and the paths available to operate it. Recovery keys can be created or deleted from the Node API when an explicit recovery workflow is needed.</p><div class="next__links"><a class="primary-link" href="/swaggerui#/Nodes.Node">Node API <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/guide/commands">Next: commands</a></div></section>
  </article>
</.page>
